<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord William Bentinck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as William Bentinck</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord William Bentinck was a British soldier and statesman. As British commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> he landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in April 1814, took the city from the French and encouraged a restored republic, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> then gave to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. As governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1828 to 1835 he abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> and promoted English-language education.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lord-william-bentinck.jpg" alt="Lord William Bentinck" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Based on painting by Sir T. Lawrence (1769 – 1s830) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bentinck_william.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Henry Cavendish-Bentinck was born in Buckinghamshire, the second son of the Prime Minister William Bentinck, 3rd Duke of Portland, and Lady Dorothy Cavendish, and was educated at Westminster School. He joined the Coldstream Guards in 1791 at the age of 16 and rose to colonel by 1798. In 1803 he was appointed Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a>. His order forbidding native troops to wear their traditional dress prompted the Vellore Mutiny of 1806, and he was recalled in 1807.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After service in the Peninsular War, Bentinck became commander of British troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1811. A Whig, he pressed for reform there: the queen, Maria Carolina, whom he exiled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, called him the ferocious beast, and a Sicilian constitution of 20 July 1812 separated legislative and executive powers and abolished feudal practices. It was abolished on 8 December 1816, though its memory influenced the Sicilian revolutions of 1820 and 1848.</p><p>In 1814 he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and on 14 March issued a proclamation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> calling on Italians to rise, in defiance of the policy of his own government, which meant to restore the earlier order with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. Genoa surrendered to him on 18 April 1814. He was ordered to hold it for the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> but instead proclaimed the restoration of the Republic of Genoa. The Prime Minister, Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, called his conduct mad, and he was recalled in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After some years in the House of Commons, Bentinck was appointed Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> in 1828. He aimed to put the loss-making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in order so that its charter would be renewed, and made cost-cutting measures that earned the lasting enmity of military men whose pay was cut. On 4 December 1829 he issued Regulation XVII, declaring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sati-practice">sati</a> illegal and punishable in criminal courts. Petitioners took the matter to the Privy Council, which upheld the ban in 1832. He also prohibited female infanticide and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifice</a>.</p><p>The English Education Act 1835 made English the medium of instruction, and English replaced Persian in the higher courts. He founded the Calcutta Medical College, the first Western medical college in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and open to all regardless of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> or creed. The Charter Act 1833 was passed in his time. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1835, refused a peerage, sat for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 17 June 1839.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lord William Bentinck” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Other stories have no support: tales of Shah Jahan mutilating his builders, of <b>Lord William Bentinck</b> planning to auction its marble, and the theories, promoted by Henry George Keene in 1879 and by William Henry Sleeman, that an Italian or a Frenchman designed it.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In April 1814 British forces under <b>Lord William Bentinck</b> landed and took Genoa from the French.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-genoa-1814">Republic of Genoa (1814)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Buckinghamshire, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.77,-0.8&amp;year=1816" aria-label="Buckinghamshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lord William Bentinck"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4958 5701h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4958 5701h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.77,-0.8&amp;year=1816">What was here in 1816? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
